            <ArticleTitle>Fasciola hepatica as a cause of jaundice after chewing khat: a case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>478-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Fasciola hepatica is a worldwide distributed zoonotic trematode incidentally infecting humans. Although often symptomatic, fascioliasis can cause a wide spectrum of disease. The diagnosis can be established by stool examination detecting ova of the parasite, although serological testing has a higher sensitivity and specificity in the acute phase of disease. This case presents a 24-year-old Somalian man admitted with jaundice and abdominal discomfort due to fascioliasis after chewing khat. The patient was treated successfully with a single dose of triclabendazole.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
